.SS ocuse commands
.TP
.B (no command)
Run the live dashboard in watch mode: a frame redrawn in place every 10
seconds (colour, severity gauges, and the spend\-burst sparkline) until
Ctrl\-C.  The frame and countdown reuse \fBcmduse\fR's in\-place redraw
contract, so a shrinking frame never scrolls.
.TP
.B \-1, \-\-once
Fetch once, print, exit.  With \fB\-\-json\fR it prints the JSON projection
instead of the text dashboard.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Machine\-readable projection (one object): \fIsource\fR, \fIgeneratedAt\fR,
\fIperiodStart\fR (and its alias \fIperiodStartAt\fR, both epoch ms),
\fIperiodEndAt\fR (the resolved end of the period in epoch ms, \fBnull\fR for
\fB\-\-window all\fR, whose range has no boundary), \fIperiodInferred\fR,
\fIaccount\fR (the three account meters from Go's usage endpoint \(em
\fIrolling\fR, \fIweekly\fR, \fImonthly\fR, each \fI{status, percent,
resetsAt}\fR with \fIresetsAt\fR in epoch ms \(em or \fBnull\fR when the
endpoint was not reached), \fItotals\fR (each of month, fiveHour,
weekly carrying cost, requests, and tokens), and the per\-model breakdown
\fImodels\fR.  A bare \fB\-\-json\fR also leaves the watch loop, so it prints
and exits rather than redrawing.
.TP
.B model [\fIid\fR]
Local usage grouped by model.  With no \fIid\fR, the full model table; with an
\fIid\fR, that model's limit, its month/5\-hour/weekly spend, and its share of
period spend.  An unused model prints that it has no usage in the period.
\fB\-\-since\fR \fIISO\fR pins the window start (an exact instant, outranking
\fB\-\-window\fR and \fB\-\-period\-start\fR).  \fB\-\-json\fR emits one object
\fI{"scope","source","sinceMs","models"}\fR whose \fImodels\fR map each id to
requests, tokensIn, tokensOut, cacheRead, costUsd and its limit; \fB\-\-csv\fR
emits \fImodel,requests,tokens_in,tokens_out,cache_read,cost_usd\fR.
.TP
.B daily
Spend per day (oldest first) over the read window (\fB\-\-days\fR), bucketed in
the display timezone (\fB\-\-tz\fR).  \fB\-\-json\fR emits
\fI{"scope","source","total","days"}\fR; \fB\-\-csv\fR emits
\fIday,requests,tokens_in,tokens_out,cache_read,cost_usd\fR with a final
\fItotal\fR row.
.TP
.B hourly
Spend per hour, same bucketing as \fBdaily\fR but over the last \fB\-\-hours\fR
N hours (default 24, max 168).  \fB\-\-json\fR emits
\fI{"scope","source","total","hours"}\fR; \fB\-\-csv\fR emits
\fIhour,requests,tokens_in,tokens_out,cache_read,cost_usd\fR with a final
\fItotal\fR row.
.TP
.B session
Spend per session, newest first (top 20).  \fB\-\-since\fR \fIISO\fR pins the
window start.  \fB\-\-json\fR emits \fI{"scope","source","sessions"}\fR (each
entry adding the last\-used instant and the distinct\-model count);
\fB\-\-csv\fR emits
\fIsession,requests,tokens_in,tokens_out,cache_read,cost_usd\fR.
.TP
.B models
The Go catalogue for the selected plan: each model's name, id, \fB$\fR/1M
in/out/cache\-read rates, the plan's monthly limit, and a \fIGRANTED\fR column.
Without \fB\-\-gated\fR every catalogued model is listed and marked;
\fB\-\-gated\fR keeps only the models the plan grants (present in the plan's
allowance map).  \fB\-\-json\fR prints one JSON object per line with keys id,
name, input, output, cacheRead, limit (null when not granted) and granted.
Needs no database; works offline.
.TP
.B plans
Print the Go/Zen catalogue: both Go plans (label, price, and each model's
monthly limit) beside the shared token rates, then a line for the Zen
pay\-as\-you\-go models.  \fB\-\-json\fR emits one object with
\fIextractedAt\fR, \fIwindows\fR (the 5\-hour/weekly/monthly shares),
\fIplans\fR (id, label, priceUsd, allowanceByModel), and the Go and Zen rate
tables.  Needs no database; works offline.
.TP
.B statusline
Print one compact line: month spend, 5\-hour spend, and the top model.
\fB\-\-json\fR prints one object: plan, period, monthSpent, monthCap,
fiveHourSpent, fiveHourCap, weeklySpent, weeklyCap, model.
.TP
.B mcp
Run an MCP stdio server; see \fBocuse mcp server\fR below.
.TP
.B \-V, \-\-version
Print \fBocuse <version>\fR and exit 0.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Print usage and exit 0.
.SS ocuse options
.TP
.B \-\-db \fIpath\fR
opencode store path.  Default \fBOPENCODE_DB\fR, else
.IR ~/.local/share/opencode/opencode.db .
Opened read\-only, so a running opencode is unaffected; a missing or
unreadable store exits 1 with a hint.  Alongside it, the \fBpi\fR
(\fI~/.pi/agent/sessions\fR, overridden by \fBPI_SESSIONS_DIR\fR) and
\fBdsh\fR (\fI~/.dsh/sessions\fR, zstd\-compressed, overridden by
\fBDSH_SESSIONS_DIR\fR) session logs are read, and only their rows on
\fBopencode-go\fR / \fBopencode\fR count: both harnesses spend the same
allowance.  The header names each client read, and \fB\-\-json\fR carries the
list as \fBclients\fR; a missing store contributes nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-plan \fIid\fR
Which Go plan to measure against: \fBgo\fR (default) or \fBgo-plus\fR.  There
is deliberately no auto\-detection: the usage endpoint reports percentages
rather than a plan id, and the local store records no subscription, so the plan
must be given.  An unknown id exits 2 and lists the known ids.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-api
Do not call OpenCode Go's usage endpoint: report the local estimate only.
Useful offline, or to compare the local windows against the account's.
.TP
.B \-\-days \fIn\fR
Days of history to read (default 120).  Bounds the
\fBdaily\fR window and the range the MCP tools scan.
.TP
.B \-\-hours \fIn\fR
Hours of history for \fBhourly\fR (default 24, max 168).
.TP
.B \-\-gated
models: keep only the models the selected plan grants.
.TP
.B \-\-window \fIw\fR
Reporting window for the dashboard and statusline: \fBmonth\fR (default, the
calendar month in UTC), \fBall\fR, or \fI<n>d\fR.  Anything else exits 2.
.TP
.B \-\-period-start \fIYYYY\-MM\-DD\fR
Pin the period start (midnight UTC) instead of deriving it from
\fB\-\-window\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-infer\-anniversary
Infer the period start from the first Go request ever seen, instead of the
calendar month.  Opt\-in, because sparse history infers badly; ignored when
\fB\-\-period-start\fR or \fB\-\-since\fR is given.  The JSON's
\fIperiodInferred\fR reports which
was used.
.TP
.B \-\-since \fIISO\fR
Window start for \fBmodel\fR/\fBsession\fR as an exact ISO\-8601 instant
(\fI2026\-08\-27T00:00:00Z\fR).  Outranks \fB\-\-window\fR and
\fB\-\-period\-start\fR; an unparseable value exits 2.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Machine\-readable output for every subcommand that has one: the dashboard
(\fB\-1\fR), \fBdaily\fR, \fBhourly\fR, \fBmodel\fR, \fBsession\fR,
\fBmodels\fR, \fBplans\fR, \fBstatusline\fR.  Never coloured.
.TP
.B \-\-csv
CSV output for \fBdaily\fR, \fBhourly\fR, \fBmodel\fR and \fBsession\fR
(the columns carry the store's fields; there is no cache\-write column).
Never coloured.  Supplying both \fB\-\-json\fR and \fB\-\-csv\fR exits 2.
.TP
.B \-\-rows \fIn\fR
Table rows to print (default 15).
.TP
.B \-\-plain
No colour and no gauges.  Colour is also dropped automatically on a
non\-terminal stdout or when \fBNO_COLOR\fR is set.
.TP
.B \-\-tz \fI\(+-HH:MM\fR
Display timezone for the \fBdaily\fR/\fBhourly\fR buckets.  Accepts
.IR +05:30 ", " -08:00 ", " +0530 .
Anything else exits 2.
.SS ocuse data and windows
When Go's usage endpoint answers, the header's three window lines are the
account's own meters (the shares and resets \fBopencode.ai/console\fR shows),
each with this store's own spend beside the server's share of the
limit \(em priced on the documented peak/off\-peak schedule, because opencode
records the off\-peak sheet for every turn and would read about 40% under the
console on a peak\-heavy day \(em the two need not agree, so the share carries its own \fIof limit\fR label
\(em and the billing period comes from the endpoint's monthly reset instant \(em
the subscription anniversary, which is why the period line reads
\fIGo billing month\fR.  No dollar denominator is printed: any ceiling
derived from one side contradicts the other \(em \fIspend / share\fR offered
\fI$40\fR against a documented \fI$80\fR monthly allowance, and the summed
per\-model limits were the old \fI$215\fR.  The burn still gets an \fIon pace
to hit cap in\fR ETA, and the monthly row \(em the one budget \(em its
\fI$X by now\fR pro\-rata figure.
Each model's table row still compares its own spend
against its monthly limit; the table's \fI5H\fR/\fIWK\fR columns are local
rolling windows (this model's spend over the last 5 hours / 7 days), and the
dashboard says so rather than letting them read as a second opinion.  Above the
table a \fITOP 3 MODELS\fR block states the period's own split: each model's
period spend and its share of all period spend, then its tokens (input +
output) and their share of all tokens (cache reads excluded).  The row table
below carries no share of its own \(em a share needs a whole, and that block is
the whole.  When the endpoint answered, the \fIperiod:\fR line also ends with
the account's monthly share, \fIN% of limit\fR, exactly as the server published
it (a whole \fI53\fR, never \fI53.0\fR).
.TP
Without the endpoint \(em no Go key, no network, or \fB\-\-no\-api\fR \(em the
window lines are a local estimate and are labelled as one: the rows carry
spend alone, since limits are per model and no account\-wide pool exists to
divide by.  Only the monthly period has a real end, so that row alone gains
a reset countdown and its \fIwindow N% elapsed\fR share; the rolling 5\-hour
and weekly rows show no countdown.  The period defaults to the calendar month, with
\fB\-\-period\-start\fR pinning the real anniversary and
\fB\-\-infer\-anniversary\fR guessing it from history.  The header splits period tokens
\fIin / out\fR and names the resolved end date.  A model whose spend
crosses its limit is flagged \fILIMIT EXCEEDED\fR.  There is no
\fISuccess N%\fR figure \(em opencode's store records no per\-request success
or error signal.  Limits and rates come from
.IR core/zen.json ,
regenerated from OpenCode's own docs sources with
.BR "bun scripts/extract\-zen.ts" .
.SS ocuse mcp server
\fBocuse mcp\fR runs an MCP stdio server (newline\-delimited JSON\-RPC on
stdin/stdout, diagnostics on stderr) exposing seven tools \(em \fIusage\fR,
\fIplans\fR, \fImodels\fR, \fIdaily\fR, \fIhourly\fR, \fIsession\fR,
\fImodel\fR \) whose output matches the corresponding subcommands.  Tool
execution errors are returned as results with \fIisError\fR true, never as
JSON\-RPC errors.  stdout carries protocol traffic only.
.SS ocuse files
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/opencode/opencode.db
Read\-only; opencode's own message store (v1 \fBmessage\fR, or v2
\fBsession_message\fR after the migration), the only per\-model cost source.
.TP
.I $OPENCODE_DB
Overrides the store path (equivalently, \fB\-\-db\fR).
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/opencode/auth.json
Read (never written) as the fallback Go key \(em the legacy v1 location of the
credential \fB/connect\fR stores; on v2 the live one is the \fBcredential\fR row
of opencode's store, which is read first.  \fB$OCUSE_API_KEY\fR and
\fB$OPENCODE_API_KEY\fR override both.
.SS arkuse
.B arkuse
tracks a Volcengine ARK Agent Plan and reports in
.BR AFP ,
the plan's own unit, never in dollars or yuan: one AFP is ¥0.002 at every
tier and a model's coefficient converts tokens into it \(em
\fIAFP = (input + cache_read + output) \(mu coef / 10,000\fR.  Cache reads are
billed; \fIreasoning\fR is not added again because it already sits inside
the output count.  Coefficients, tier gates, promo dates and the plan table
are hand\-maintained in
.IR core/ark.json ,
because the publisher documents them as prose.
